// Where a Text lands in its rect, what colour it is, and its first argument.
//
// The anchor's horizontal half decides where the string starts: `left` at the
// rect's left edge, `center` on its middle, `right` at its right. The vertical
// half is ignored -- a single line is centred in its rect, which is what every
// one of these has meant since the first layout.

ui_tx_off(int x, int w, int n, string txt) {
  int ui_ax_v = ui_ax(txt, w);
  int ui_al_v = ui_al(txt);
  int ret_i = x + ui_ax_v - txt_align(n, ui_al_v);
} return int ret_i;
(0, 100, 10, "center"):(45)
(0, 100, 10, "left"):(4)

ui_tx(int nd, int x, int w, int n) {
  int ast_ib_v = ast_ib(nd);
  string ast_tx_v = ast_tx(ast_ib_v);
  int ret_i = ui_tx_off(x, w, n, ast_tx_v);
} return int ret_i;
given ui_leaf_t_setup (3, 0, 100, 5):(4)
given ui_leaf_t_setup (3, 10, 100, 5):(14)

// Centred on the rect at the *UI's* scale, so a label stays in the middle of its
// box as the view zooms rather than creeping toward the top of it.
//
// The 8 here was the font's cell height, spelled as a literal because for as long
// as the face was 8x8 it was also the cell width and the advance. It is
// txt_cellh() now: with the 8x16 face the literal put every label half a line
// high in its box, which reads as a layout bug rather than as a font change --
// and it is the one place in the engine that centred text vertically at all.
ui_ty(int y, int h) {
  int ret_i = y + (h - txt_cellh() * (import ui_ts)[0] / 1000) / 2;
} return int ret_i;
given txt_init given ui_row_t_setup (10, 20):(12)
given txt_init given ui_row_t_setup (0, 16):(0)
